A||N||11||2xsess||Bench Testing an A/C compressor||||5||
Z||000000||2xsess||10-10-2000||11:42 AM||||I was just wondering if it was possible. Anyone know how?<P>TIA<P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR>85GT||||reg||5
Z||000001||Carrolles||10-10-2000||12:20 PM||||Turn it on.  If the bench gets cool, it is working.  jk<P>Seriously,  I don't know.  Try the  <A HREF="http://WWW.Airconditioning.com" TARGET=_blank>WWW.Airconditioning.com</A>  forum.<P>Let us know what you find out.<P>Carroll||||reg||1
Z||000002||Carrolles||10-11-2000||09:50 AM||||Try this link instead.<BR> <A HREF="http://WWW.Aircondition.com/" TARGET=_blank>http://WWW.Aircondition.com/</A> <P>Sorry,<P>Carroll||||reg||1
Z||000003||EdsB52||10-11-2000||10:13 AM||||I didn't look at the web site, but all you can easily do is run 12 volts to the clutch wires and hand spin the pulley to see if it engages. If there were an elaborate contraption that spun the pulley at operating rpm while it applied freon, ran it through a condesor, drier, and evaporator, then I guess it would be possible. Unless it were a $2000 compressor, I think it would be cost prohibitive.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Zorba||10-11-2000||10:18 AM|||| Can you buy just the lines in case the got damaged? I think mine are leaking somewhere near the compressor...||||reg||1
Z||000005||theogre||10-11-2000||04:34 PM||||There's nothing you can bench test on it but the clutch. unless you are going to build a whole AC system on the bench.<P><BR>As long as the aluminum hardware is ok it's posible to make up new rubber sections. Trick is finding someone to do it.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 10-11-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||sdm1964||10-11-2000||10:17 PM||||We have a compressor in the Gt we bought for parts, His is locked up and we were wondering if their was a way to test it before putting it on.||||reg||1
Z||000007||theogre||10-12-2000||08:58 AM||||you can make sure it's not locked up. turn the shaft with a wrench in the same direction as normal. That won't tell you it's good just that it's not jammed.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000008||mrfixit58||10-12-2000||10:10 AM||||I have had some luck testing A/C compressors using a very simple method. Cover the holes with your fingertips then turn the compressor. If you feel a strong vacuum on one finder and pressure on the other, AND the holes are clear of particles, there is a chance the compressor is still good. I've used this method several times with other style compressors and have yet to be dissapointed. Or, maybe I've just been lucky.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Good Luck,<BR>Roy  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||4
Z||000009||Linx||10-12-2000||09:27 PM||||Yeah- i was gonna suggest the same; the finger over the hole trick.  It's reliable.  A well lubed compressor can be turned with your hand.  Beware the oil being ejected from the ports, tho'.||||reg||1
Z||000010||EdsB52||10-12-2000||09:50 PM||||Another thing. I don't mean to be elementary, but make sure and add oil to the compressor before running it in the car. Make sure it is the correct type of oil for the type of freon used when the compressor was used in its previously application......probably R-12.||||reg||1
